Hi Anthony,

Going through your script:

1. Don't do ctmm analysis in lon-lat. You want a locally flat projection.
2. Not a big deal, but I added fast=FALSE to variogram() since you had irregular VHF data. This removes some bias stemming from irregular sampling.
3. I had the OU model winning model selection, but in your code you revert back to an OUF ctmm.fit model after model selection. This had no discernible effect on the UD though.

It looks like what happened is that your confidence intervals blew up, because the Hessian of the log-likelihood function was poorly calculated, because default arguments of in the numDeriv package took your square-degree "area" to be within tolerance of zero, because your area was on the order of 10^-10 square degrees. I'm not sure this can happen with normal usage (maybe if somebody tracks bacterium?), so for now I'm just going to throw an error into as.telemetry when users try to use lon-lat projection.

Best,
Chris

